{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Elohim blessed, etc.</b> One must know what the blessing is. Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 11:2): He blessed it with the manna, etc. But their words of blessed memory are only a hint in the verse, whereas the plain sense does not speak of any of the future times." "And the correct understanding of the verse's meaning is this: since this world requires the sustaining influx, which is the aspect of eating and drinking and human needs — all of which are attained through physical toil and exertion, and are of the profane — when HaShem wished to sanctify the seventh day, He first blessed it so that nothing would be lacking in it. Even though the good of the lower world is not attained through separation and holiness but rather through weekday occupation, nevertheless He blessed it so that good would not be lacking. It further has, beyond the other days, a double portion of bread for three meals and greater delights — and this is its blessing, and it is the opposite of what one would expect. And according to what the Zohar (Yitro 88a) explains, that on the day of Shabbat the influx of all six days of the work cascades down, the verse's saying <b>and He blessed</b> may be understood in this vein, for in it HaShem commanded the blessing for the vitality of the worlds." "<b>For in it He rested, etc.</b> One must know the intention of the verse in this statement. <b>It appears</b> to explain according to what we have written above, that the world was lacking sustenance until Shabbat came and the world stood. But one must raise a difficulty here: those days before Shabbat arrived — on what did the world stand? Just as He sufficed to sustain it during those days without the soul of Shabbat, He could have continued indefinitely." "<b>Indeed,</b> we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, disclosed the matter in what He said (Exodus 20:11) in the Ten Commandments: ״כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים עָשָׂה ה׳ אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc. — thereby He revealed that at the time of creation HaShem did not create power in the world to endure beyond six days, for a reason known to Him and also known to those who know the truth. And HaShem devised wisely and created one day known to HaShem, and on that day HaShem in turn bestows a soul upon the world — the measure of sustenance — for another six days, and so on in this manner. And were it not for this day, the world would have been destroyed at the completion of six days and would have returned to תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ, and HaShem would have needed to prepare it a second time. But through the mediation of Shabbat the world stands. And this is what the verse says: <b>for in it</b> — meaning: through the mediation of this day He rested from all His work. And He explained what the work is — it is the act of creation itself, for He rested not only from the rectification, which is the making, but also from the creation. And I have already written above that the creation is something from nothing, which is what was created on the first day by a single utterance, while the making is the rectification that He rectified each day. And through the mediation of the day of Shabbat He bestows upon the totality of the worlds a spirit that sustains them for another six days." "And according to our explanation we find satisfaction in one statement that they of blessed memory made (Shabbat 119b): ״Whoever sanctifies… and says וַיְכֻלּוּ etc. — it is as though he became a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the act of Creation״ — end quote. But their words seem far-fetched and strained according to appearances. Who has heard such a thing — that a person should come and become a partner in a matter whose work has already been completed? After the act of creation there is nothing, and in what would he become a partner? Furthermore, what is this great deed through which one attains this attainment? <b>Furthermore,</b> even if this deed were extremely great, whence do they derive to say this thing — that he becomes a partner? And if it were for the sake of magnifying the reward, they should have magnified his reward by granting him a share in multitudes (Isaiah 53:12) and length of days and strength and wealth, etc. Whence did they find to extend the language on the supernal side, to say that he becomes a partner with Him in the act of Creation?" "And that which I have explained — truth shall a wise man utter, and the word of HaShem in their mouth is truth, for a fitting reason — namely, that the Sabbath is what sustains the world for all six days, and after six days another Sabbath comes and revives it and sustains it for yet another six days. And you should know that the existence of the Sabbath in the world is its sustenance through those who observe it, for if there are none who keep the Sabbath, there is no Sabbath. And you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 151a): ״Profane one Sabbath on his behalf so that he may keep many Sabbaths,״ and if there is no keeper of the Sabbath, what does this accomplish? And therefore, ״Whoever sanctifies the Sabbath״ etc. — meaning, sustains and guards its holiness; through this agency the Sabbath exists, and through its agency the world is sustained. You have been shown to know that it is what sustains the world, and you have no greater partner than this according to law and according to halakhah. And you should know that from the day Elohim created man, the world has never lacked the thing that upholds it — a righteous one, the foundation of the world, who is a keeper of the Sabbath. For man was a keeper of the Sabbath — peace be upon him (Genesis Rabbah 22); and after him arose his son Shet, for he was a complete righteous one; and after him many righteous ones; and when this one fell, already there came the sun who kept the Sabbath — Methuselah etc., Noah, Shem, Abraham etc. — and from there the observance of the Sabbath never ceased from Israel; and even in Egypt they were keepers of Sabbaths, as they said of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 1:32) — and this is correct." "And although we wrote above in the commentary on the verse ״וַיְכֻלּוּ״ (Genesis 2:1) that the sustaining of the world is through the yearning to receive the surrounding light and the inner light in the hidden realms, this does not contradict our words here, for without the Sabbath there is no grasp in the world to recognize, to yearn, and to be consumed with longing toward the Creator — and understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {3 "The phrase וְנִתְחַכֵּם ה׳ ('and HaShem devised wisely') is rendered literally; the root חכם in hithpa'el/po'el here carries the sense of divine wisdom applied to creation's design."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:40.011653159Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}